  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday night through Monday)\nIssued at 300 AM EDT Tue Aug 25 2026\n\nNot much change in the extended periods. The models are generally in \ngood agreement through the weekend. Troughing over the Southeast for \nthe end of the work week with ridging building into the weekend.\n\nBeginning Wed afternoon/evening, under the influence of the trough, \nchance to likely PoPs will prevail across the area through Friday. A \nfew waves within the trough may diurnally enhance convection each \nafternoon and lead to the potential for some isolated strong to \nsevere storms. The models are showing a boundary developing over GA \nWed night/Thu morning as a first main wave moves SE out of the Mid \nMS river valley and into W GA. This boundary stays nearly stationary \nthrough Friday so we could see a fairly active Thu/Fri. Precipitable \nwater values near the boundary stay around the 2\" range beginning \nlate Wed through Sat. This is most likely why WPC has placed a good \nportions of the state under a marginal risk of excessive rainfall \nfor days 4 and 5.  Would not be surprised if SPC put us under a \nMarginal risk for severe storms as we get closer to Thu/Fri. Will \nneed to see how the models handle things over the next few runs to \nkeep and eye on severe/excessive rain trends.\n\nMoisture is forecasted to remain plentiful through the weekend, but \nthe Bermuda ridge is expected to begin to push back in across the \nSERN U.S. This should help to cap the stronger to severe \ngrowth aspect of diurnal convective development Sat/Sun.",
